       Column (2) totals 2775 members across the eight still-unset projects (the 107 in the seven set
       projects were WRITTEN, not suppressed, which is why their column-(2) price is now zero: N-1 paid 12,
       N-2 paid this project's 95). 2775 is the number that is an owner decision and not an implementer
       one, and it is why those eight stay unset: the only honest ways to set them are to write 2775 doc
       comments or to disable a code, and the first is a product decision about what those surfaces promise
       while the second is forbidden here. A doc comment invented for a domain field nobody has decided the
       meaning of is a new lie, not a fix.

       🔴 N-2 DID NOT WEAKEN THAT SENTENCE, and it is worth saying why, because "one project just wrote 95
       of them" is the obvious way to misread this row. The 95 were payable because every one of them had a
       MEANING RECOVERABLE FROM THIS REPOSITORY — the host's Normalizer maps every DeviceReading field onto
       a named field of one of the three documented ingest payloads, the doc-28 reader fixes the accepted
       token sets, and the alarm evaluator gives Degraded/Down their operator-visible difference. That is a
       property of THIS project, not of the number 95, and nothing here establishes that the remaining 2775
       have the same property. The test is still "can this sentence be recovered", never "is this project
       cheap".

       🔴 AND "DISABLE A CODE" IS TWO DIFFERENT DECISIONS THAT DIFFER BY 448 MEMBERS. Both are forbidden to
       an implementer here; they are NOT interchangeable to whoever authorises one:
         (a) ASSEMBLY-WIDE — `<NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>` on St4i.EdgeCore. Silences all 543. That
             project then asserts documentation coverage NOWHERE, including on the 448 members that are our
             own source and that nothing prevents anyone from documenting.
         (b) PATH-SCOPED — an .editorconfig section matching only the vendored file
             (`dotnet_diagnostic.CS1591.severity = none` under a pattern for St4iDeviceClient.cs). Silences
             95 and LEAVES THE OTHER 448 ASSERTED, which is the whole difference: it exempts the file nobody
             here may edit and keeps the check on everything we own.
       (b) is strictly the narrower instrument and is what "St4i.EdgeCore is blocked" actually argues for.
       🔴 One thing to settle before relying on it, flagged because I did NOT measure it: this file enters
       the compilation through a `Compile Include` from OUTSIDE the project cone, and .editorconfig sections
       match a file's REAL path, not its `Link` metadata — so such a section has to be scoped to the real
       path under examples/device-client/csharp/, and that should be verified by running it rather than
       reasoned about. Everything else in this block was measured; that sentence was not.

       🔴 AND IF ONLY ONE PROJECT EVER MOVES, MOVE St4i.Connector.Abstractions. ✅ DONE — N-2 took exactly
       this decision, and the three facts below are the reason it was taken rather than a proposal awaiting
