       116 because nothing was left over. Kept in the present tense rather than rewritten, because it is
       also the argument for what to do next: the same question ("is this surface's correctness worth more
       than its price?") is still unasked for the eight rows that remain. Put here rather than in a report
       because this is where someone deciding will be standing. Three facts, and they point the same way
       for once:
         * It is the ONLY documented surface in this tree with readers outside this organisation — it is the
           contract a third-party driver author compiles against and reads first (zero PackageReference, zero
           ProjectReference, deliberately, so they can).
         * By the argument this whole block makes, it is therefore the surface whose doc-comment correctness
           matters MOST — and N-1's scope left it unguarded. The six were selected by what assertion (2)
           COST, and cost correlates only loosely with what a comment's correctness is worth. That row was
           where the two came apart hardest, and it was the honest weakness of N-1's scope, not a footnote
           to it. It is closed.
         * It was also a CHEAP such move — 95 members against the 2775 that remain unset (it was NOT the
